Z||000000||Xtreme Fiero's||09-17-2000||09:23 PM||||<BR> I will be geting a used duke from kick hill prety soon, It there any advantage to geting an 85 or 86 duke over the 84? 84-86 are the same right and when did they go to a roller cam. Thanks<P>PS. I will be useing the stock 84 ECM & 4:10 4-speed||||reg||5
Z||000001||theogre||09-17-2000||11:29 PM||||I don't know for sure when roller cams started but that's what you want if you can get it. My notes indicate 86 was roller, but because of engine replacements rollers can show up in 84's or 85's. You definatly want the 86 if available just because it's hopefully newer. <P>all three years are the same as far as the ECM and all that care.<P>The only quick test to be sure it's a roller cam I can think of is to take off the valve cover and pull a push rod out to check the length against a known one. The roller cams use shorter pushrods than the flat ones because the roller lifters are taller.<P>One thing to do while it's out is change the cam gears. That's the one engine job that's a real _____ when it's in the car. The cam gears hold up better in the later engines but I'd definatly do this while it's out.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Xtreme Fiero's||09-18-2000||05:55 PM||||Awsome, Thanks||||reg||1
